  "narrative": {
    "intro": "North Dakota has received $644M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 2,141 awards with an average award size of $301K, and obligations fell 21.6% year over year. Obligations by fiscal year show peaks and troughs: FY21 $780M, FY22 $973M, FY23 $803M, FY24 $980M, FY25 $821M, and FY26 $644M. The Department of Health and Human Services is the largest buying agency with $281M, followed by the Department of Defense with $170M and the Department of Transportation with $86.1M. NORIDIAN HEALTHCARE SOLUTIONS, LLC is the largest vendor at $262M, followed by WALSH FEDERAL LLC at $74.4M and PACKET DIGITAL LLC at $36.8M. Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers is the largest industry by obligations at $225M, followed by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $115M. This snapshot covers only contract obligations and reflects partial fiscal-year totals through FY2026 to date. Use these figures to prioritize outreach, bid planning, and channeling proposal resources toward the largest agencies, vendors, and industry categories in the state.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why did obligations fall year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 21.6% year over year. FY26 obligations are $644M compared with FY25 obligations of $821M and FY24 obligations of $980M. The decline follows FY24 levels that were higher than earlier years such as FY23 $803M and FY21 $780M. These figures are fiscal-year to date totals reflecting partial current-year obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the top buyers and vendors this fiscal year?",
        "body": "The Department of Health and Human Services is the top buyer with $281M, followed by the Department of Defense at $170M and the Department of Transportation at $86.1M. NORIDIAN HEALTHCARE SOLUTIONS, LLC is the top vendor at $262M, followed by WALSH FEDERAL LLC at $74.4M and PACKET DIGITAL LLC at $36.8M. Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers is the leading industry with $225M, and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction follows with $115M."
      }
    ]
  },
